{"name":"Windsurfing","hex":"#3a7099","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"windsurfing","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A marine sport in which one stands on a floating board (typically 2 - 3 meters in length) to which a sail is attached. The board is steered by tilting the sail or banking the board. Some windsurfers use large waves to perform jumps and other stunts."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of windsurf"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/windsurfing"}],"entries":[{"title":"Windsurfing","blocks":[{"text":"Windsurfing is a wind-propelled water sport that is a combination of sailing and surfing. It is also referred to as \"sailboarding\" and \"boardsailing\", and emerged in the late 1960s from the Californian aerospace and surf culture. Windsurfing gained a popular following across Europe and North America by the late 1970s and had achieved significant global popularity by the 1980s. Windsurfing became an Olympic sport in 1984."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Hood_river_windsurfers_20060701_0759.jpeg/330px-Hood_river_windsurfers_20060701_0759.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windsurfing"},{"title":"Windsurfing (advertisement)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Windsurfing\" was a political advertisement commissioned by Republican president George W. Bush's campaign during the 2004 United States presidential election. The ad, which shows Democratic nominee John Kerry windsurfing while a voiceover states that his views shift \"whichever way the wind blows\", criticized Kerry's alternating positions on the Iraq war, a bill that directed supplemental aid to U.S. soldiers, education reform, and the price of Medicare premiums."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windsurfing_(advertisement)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/9f/Darby_sailboard%2C_Published_Popular_Science%2C_August_1965.gif/250px-Darby_sailboard%2C_Published_Popular_Science%2C_August_1965.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/9f/Darby_sailboard%2C_Published_Popular_Science%2C_August_1965.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/9f/Darby_sailboard%2C_Published_Popular_Science%2C_August_1965.gif/250px-Darby_sailboard%2C_Published_Popular_Science%2C_August_1965.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Darby sailboard, Popular Science, 1965","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADarby_sailboard%2C_Published_Popular_Science%2C_August_1965.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Windsurfpatent.jpg/500px-Windsurfpatent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Windsurfpatent.jpg/1280px-Windsurfpatent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Windsurfpatent.jpg/500px-Windsurfpatent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Drake windsurfer, patent drawing, 1967","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWindsurfpatent.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Windsurfing_at_Sandbanks%2C_Poole_Harbour%2C_England.jpg/500px-Windsurfing_at_Sandbanks%2C_Poole_Harbour%2C_England.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Windsurfing_at_Sandbanks%2C_Poole_Harbour%2C_England.jpg/500px-Windsurfing_at_Sandbanks%2C_Poole_Harbour%2C_England.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Windsurfing in the late evening on a longboard at Sandbanks in the 1980s (Poole Harbour, England)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWindsurfing_at_Sandbanks%2C_Poole_Harbour%2C_England.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Sail_Size_Selection_Table.png/500px-Sail_Size_Selection_Table.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Sail_Size_Selection_Table.png/500px-Sail_Size_Selection_Table.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ideal sail size (m2) for different wind speeds and rider weights (recreational level). The red values indicate sail sizes that are unpractical or not available.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASail_Size_Selection_Table.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Carve_Gybe.jpg/500px-Carve_Gybe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Carve_Gybe.jpg/500px-Carve_Gybe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Carving a jibe: the sailor is turning to the left, just past dead downwind. To finish the turn, he will jibe the sail by releasing with his left hand so that the sail flips around.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACarve_Gybe.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Windsurfing_waterstart_-_Rising_up.jpeg/500px-Windsurfing_waterstart_-_Rising_up.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Windsurfing_waterstart_-_Rising_up.jpeg/500px-Windsurfing_waterstart_-_Rising_up.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Windsurfer being pulled up onto the board during a water-start","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F